## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of the Command pattern, undo/redo implementation, and state management in a minimal system.

## Key Concepts

- Command pattern with execute/undo methods
- Receiver (TextEditor) and Command objects
- History stack for undo/redo operations
- State capture for reversible operations
- Edge cases: boundary checking, no-op commands

## Code Example

```javascript
class Editor {
  constructor() { this.text = ''; }

  insert(pos, ch) {
    this.text = this.text.slice(0, pos) + ch + this.text.slice(pos);
  }

  delete(pos, len) {
    const removed = this.text.slice(pos, pos + len);
    this.text = this.text.slice(0, pos) + this.text.slice(pos + len);
    return removed;
  }
}

class InsertCommand {
  constructor(editor, pos, ch) {
    this.editor = editor;
    this.pos = pos;
    this.ch = ch;
  }

  execute() {
    this.editor.insert(this.pos, this.ch);
  }

  undo() {
    this.editor.delete(this.pos, 1);
  }
}

class History {
  constructor() {
    this.undoStack = [];
    this.redoStack = [];
  }

  execute(command) {
    command.execute();
    this.undoStack.push(command);
    this.redoStack = [];
  }

  undo() {
    if (this.undoStack.length === 0) return;
    const command = this.undoStack.pop();
    command.undo();
    this.redoStack.push(command);
  }
}

// Example: Insert 'A' at position 0, then undo
const editor = new Editor();
const history = new History();
const insertA = new InsertCommand(editor, 0, 'A');

history.execute(insertA); // text = 'A'
history.undo(); // text = ''
```

## Implementation Notes

- Commands capture all state needed for undo
- History maintains separate undo/redo stacks
- Each operation is atomic and reversible
- Position validation ensures safe operations

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to extend system behavior at runtime, wire loosely coupled components, and reason about composition and short-circuiting in a production-grade policy engine.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy: encapsulate each policy as a separate evaluation strategy.
- Registry: dynamic registration and lookup of policies by name.
- Composition: PolicySet aggregates multiple policies and enforces all to pass (short-circuit on deny).
- Isolation: policies run independently to avoid cascading failures.

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Context { user: string; resource: string; action: string; time: Date }
interface Policy { name: string; evaluate(ctx: Context): boolean }
class PolicyRegistry { private map = new Map<string, Policy>(); register(p: Policy){ this.map.set(p.name, p); } get(name: string){ return this.map.get(name); } }
class PolicySet { constructor(private registry: PolicyRegistry, private active: string[] = []) {} addPolicy(name: string){ this.active.push(name); } evaluate(ctx: Context): boolean { for (const name of this.active){ const p = this.registry.get(name); if (p && !p.evaluate(ctx)) return false; } return true } }
class RBACPolicy implements Policy { name = 'rbac'; evaluate(ctx: Context){ return ctx.user === 'admin' || (ctx.user === 'user' && ctx.action === 'read'); } }
class TimePolicy implements Policy { name = 'time'; evaluate(ctx: Context){ const hour = ctx.time.getUTCHours(); return hour >= 9 && hour <= 17; } }

const reg = new PolicyRegistry(); reg.register(new RBACPolicy()); reg.register(new TimePolicy());
const ps = new PolicySet(reg, ['rbac','time']);
const ok = ps.evaluate({ user:'user', resource:'doc', action:'read', time: new Date()});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you support asynchronous policy checks (external lookups) in the same framework?
- How would you handle policy conflicts and define prioritization or weighting between policies?

